{
  "dom_id": "question-183761",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{n}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2n & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nfor all $n\\in N.$",
  "worked_solution": "",
